{"question": "A 78-year-old man is brought to the emergency department because of a 1-day history of painful enlarging bruises and skin ulceration over his thighs and external genitalia. He has type 2 diabetes mellitus, mitral regurgitation, and atrial fibrillation. Three days ago, he was started on treatment with warfarin. His only other medications are metformin and lisinopril. His temperature is 37.8\u00b0C (100.0\u00b0F), pulse is 108/min and irregularly irregular, and blood pressure is 155/89 mm Hg. Examination of the skin shows large purpura, hemorrhagic bullae, and areas of skin necrosis over his anterior legs, gluteal region, and penis. This patient is most likely to benefit from treatment with which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Hyperbaric oxygen", "B": "Protein C concentrate", "C": "Argatroban", "D": "Tranexamic acid", "E": "Vancomycin\n\""}, "pred_answer": "C", "gold_answer": "B", "question_domains": ["Emergency Medicine", "Dermatology", "Hematology", "Cardiology", "Endocrinology"], "option_domains": ["Hemostasis and Coagulation Disorders", "Infection and Wound Management"], "question_analyses": {"Emergency Medicine": "The patient described is most likely suffering from a condition known as warfarin-induced skin necrosis (WISN). This is a rare but serious complication of anticoagulant therapy, particularly with warfarin, which can occur within the first few days of starting the medication. The symptoms include painful skin lesions, purpura, and necrosis, which are consistent with the patient's presentation of painful enlarging bruises, skin ulceration, and necrosis over his thighs and external genitalia. ### Key Aspects of the Condition:\n1. **Timing of Symptoms**: The symptoms began shortly after starting warfarin, which is a critical clue pointing towards WISN. 2. **Clinical Presentation**: The presence of large purpura, hemorrhagic bullae, and areas of skin necrosis over the anterior legs, gluteal region, and penis is highly indicative of WISN.